-- Q306: Audit Sampling [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Under AU-C 530.A23, in monetary-unit sampling the sampling interval is calculated by dividing the total recorded dollar value of the population by the desired sample size. For this engagement, $4,500,000 / 45 = $100,000 interval, meaning every 100,000th dollar is a selection point.
Wrong (A): Dividing population size (number of items) by sample size would produce an item-based interval, not the dollar-based interval required by MUS methodology.
Wrong (C): Standard deviation divided by confidence level is a formula used in classical variables sampling, not monetary-unit sampling.
Wrong (D): Expected misstatement divided by tolerable misstatement is a risk ratio used in planning, not a formula for calculating the MUS sampling interval.$EXPL$
